# GitHub Actions: publish and certify a new ordinary Latest release.
# Push the code first. The workflows must be registered on the default branch.
# Configure the release signing secret/variable described in docs/releases.md.

Available manual workflow: _Publish new Latest release
Workflow file: .github/workflows/_release-latest.yml

Repository -> Actions -> _Publish new Latest release -> Run workflow.
Select the branch containing the complete candidate; one dispatch pins every
stage to that commit. The workflow name, run title and filename start with "_".

Options:
- version: blank uses the application version plus a timestamp.
- linux_baseline: bookworm-sdk (default), or ubuntu-22.04 for x86_64.
- linux_runner / arm_runner / windows_runner: organization H pools by default.
  For standard runners choose ubuntu-24.04 / ubuntu-24.04-arm / windows-2022.
  Standard hosted runners are free for public repositories; private-repository
  allowances and billing vary. Larger runners are billed, including for public
  repositories. L pools and the Linux M pool are also available.
- maintain_base: none (default), linux, windows or both. Select maintenance only
  when needed: source=auto reuses an existing recipe and builds a missing one.
  Ordinary stages require the matching prepared base recipes; they do not
  silently build missing dependencies.
  The TUI/framebuffer recipes add ncurses/SDL2 to Linux and SDL2 to Windows;
  maintain both bases once for these new recipe identities.
- sanitizer_smoke / sanitizer_realtime: optional extra instrumented coverage,
  both off by default. All ordinary full regression checks remain required.

The workflow calls the existing workflows below: selected base maintenance,
full native and SDK checks in parallel, ordinary publication, then full
certification of the exact published binaries. It always uses devfast=false,
experiment=false and publish=true. The final job verifies that GitHub Latest
is the new tag/release ID and matches this run's source, inventory and report.
All six new bundles include CLI, GUI, TUI and framebuffer binaries. Independent
frontend regression jobs run alongside the existing suites. Debian, Arch and
Gentoo packages retain both additional interfaces and their manuals.

The previous Latest remains selected until all candidate assets and successful
certification reports are ready. Certification pivots Latest in one release
metadata update; it never moves, deletes or copies the previous release's assets.
Separate client requests are not one atomic snapshot. Avoid independent manual
promotions during this run; a competing promotion can fail final verification.

Wait for the entire workflow to succeed, then review its linked certification
report and warnings. Record the tag, run ID and limits in docs/validation.md.
First-time signing setup, CLI examples and details: docs/releases.md.

# Manual alternatives for troubleshooting or resuming individual stages.
# Select the same branch for every run; keep it unchanged.
# Wait for each run to succeed before continuing. Leave other inputs at defaults.

1. Maintain base SDK (sdk-base.yml)                         [optional]
   Run with platform=linux, then with platform=windows.
   Keep source=auto and publish=true.
   Skip either run if its current dependency recipe is already in base.

2. Focused checks and full native regression (ci.yml)
   devfast=false (unchecked).

3. Application checks with the base SDK (sdk.yml)
   devfast=false (unchecked).

4. Manual portable release (release.yml)
   experiment=false (uncheck it); publish=true (checked).
   Leave version blank to use the application version plus a timestamp.
   Copy the new release tag from the run summary.

5. Certify published release (certify.yml)
   release_tag=the new tag from step 4; devfast=false (unchecked).

# A successfully certified ordinary release becomes Latest, with public
# Linux/Windows binaries, Linux packages and retained build dependencies.
